          Second episode of PerfEco Institutions. The first set out the principle: write the project before looking for who will pay for it. This one gives the tool: seven questions, each scored from 0 to 5.
        </p>

        <h2>Why score your project</h2>

        <p>
          When you have carried a project for a long time, it is hard to say exactly where it stands. Everyone keeps their own picture of it, and the pictures differ from one person to the next. A score forces an answer, and it gets everyone to agree on two things: what already exists, and what is still to be written.
        </p>

        <h2>The rules</h2>

        <p>
          Each question gets a score from 0 to 5. Score the project as it is written today, not as you hope it will be.
        </p>

        <ul>
          <li><strong>0 to 1: nothing written.</strong> The answer exists only in the heads of a few people.</li>
          <li><strong>2 to 3: half written.</strong> The answer exists, but it changes depending on who gives it.</li>
          <li><strong>4 to 5: written and dated.</strong> The answer fits on one page, with a name and a date.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Questions 1 to 3: the project itself</h2>

        <ol>
          <li><strong>Who it serves.</strong> A described need and a number of people concerned. Score 1: nobody has counted the beneficiaries.</li>
          <li><strong>Where it stands.</strong> Studies, land, permits. Score 1: everything is still to be done. Score 5: the documents exist and are kept in one place.</li>
          <li><strong>Who carries it.</strong> A name that decides and signs. Score 1: everyone thinks it is someone else.</li>
        </ol>

        <h2>Questions 4 to 7: the money and what follows</h2>

        <ol start="4">
          <li><strong>Who pays what.</strong> Your share, the other funders' shares, and the years after the project goes live. Score 1: only the total is known.</li>
          <li><strong>What the funding scheme accepts.</strong> The rules of the aid fit the project. Score 1: nobody has read the rules in full.</li>
          <li><strong>How you will know it works.</strong> A starting figure, an end figure, a date. Score 1: the text says "improve", with no figure.</li>
          <li><strong>Who has the time to do it.</strong> Names, hours, a calendar. Score 1: the plan counts on everyone's spare time.</li>
        </ol>

        <p>
          The seventh question is easy to skip, because it is about people more than about the project. Yet a funded project takes hours of work: who has them?
        </p>

        <div class="bg-gray-50 border-l-4 border-teal rounded-r-xl p-6 my-8">
          <p class="font-semibold text-gray-800 mb-1">Diagnostic question</p>
          <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm">On the project you talk about most, which is the lowest of your seven scores? And who on your team has the hours to raise it?</p>
        </div>

        <h2>Score it twice, separately</h2>

        <p>
          Ask two people who know the project to score it, each on their own, then compare. A gap of two points on a question means the answer is not written down anywhere. That is a useful signal, and it is better to find it before the funder does.
        </p>

        <p>
          Do not average the seven scores: an average of 3 can hide a 0 and a 5. The lowest score is the one that counts.
        </p>

        <h2>What a low score means</h2>

        <p>
          You have already answered several of these questions, without scoring them. You were right to carry this project, and a low score does not call that into question. It only tells you which page has not been written yet.
        </p>

        <h2>Our advice: one score at a time</h2>

        <p>
          Work only on the lowest score. One well-written page moves the project forward more than seven pages that are only started. Write the answer in ten lines, with a name and a date, then score yourself again.
        </p>

        <h2>What PerfEco Institutions does</h2>

        <p>
          PerfEco Institutions supports municipalities, provinces, public bodies and businesses, from the first page of the project to the last supporting document. We offer a confidential review of your projects, in 60 to 90 minutes: we score the seven questions together, project by project, then choose a pilot project with its action plan.
        </p>

        <p>
          No firm can guarantee that funding will be granted. What you keep in every case: a mature project, a costed financing plan and a reusable dashboard.
        </p>

